\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Sparked By Banks' Dollar Sales \b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1999\par 15:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-selling by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked a mild \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday afternoon, with the bank selling Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bank might have bought around $25 million. \par \par \uc2  They added that on Monday and Tuesday the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  just a few minutes before the market's 1200 GMT close, thus limiting its lira injection to the market and interest rate volatility that might start with the liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lira's depreciation rate. The bank maintains a lira depreciation of about 4% to 4.5% against the dollar/Euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the commercial banks' dollar-selling was driven by the need to meet lira demand. Tuesday's Turkish Treasury bond auction - where TRL1.2 quadrillion of paper was sold - fueled the lira demand. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the dollar closed at TRL322,550, up from Monday's close of TRL321,800. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay; +90 212 512 5017; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-01-99 \par \par \uc2  1403GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv1c008ng\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
