\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Banks' Dollar Sales Continues \b0\par\par\uc2 193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1999\par 14:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-selling by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked two major rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday afternoon, with the bank selling Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lira's depreciation rate. Traders said the daily depreciation rate increased to 0.14% after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slightly higher than in the last few trading sessions, and indicating a monthly depreciation rate of 4% to 4.5% against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders are mixed about the monthly target with some seeing depreciation at below 4%, based on monthly inflation expectations, but the target should be clearer next week. \par \par \uc2  Banks are selling dollars to fund lira assets and markets have been bullish expecting a new government. \par \par \uc2  Democratic Left Party leader Bulent Ecevit Thursday received the mandate to form the government. He said his new cabinet will take over on Friday. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the dollar closed trading at TRL318,600, up from Wednesday's close of TRL317,200. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay; +90 212 231 3355; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-01-99 \par \par \uc2  1343GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv17004q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
