\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  -2: Sparked By Banks' Dollar Sales \b0\par\par\uc2 137 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1998\par 14:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Dollar-selling by commercial banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday afternoon, with the bank selling Turkish lira (TRL) for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the lira's depreciation rate. \par \par \uc2  The bank is seen keeping a daily 1.3% to 1.5% depreciation against the dollar/Deutsche mark \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the commercial banks' dollar-selling was driven by the need to meet lira demand. In general, dollar selling demand in the market indicates relative calm and high lira rates. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the dollar closed at TRL302,570, up from Monday's close at TRL301,310. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay +90 212 512 5017; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 01-12-98 \par \par \uc2  1332GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917duc101oqd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
