\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's Bank/Buys Lira -2: Markets In Sharp Decline \b0\par\par\uc2 225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 14:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (Dow Jones)--Heavy dollar demand from commercial banks prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times in Thursday's session, with the bank buying Turkish lira for dollars. \par \par \uc2  At the time of the third round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , banks' dollar bids were at TRL278,820 and higher. Shortly after that round, bids again rose to TRL279,000. From this level, the bank staged a fourth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bank is keeping a daily depreciation rate of about 0.09%, corresponding to a monthly 2.5% to 2.6% against the dollar/Deutsche mark \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the heavy dollar demand is coupled with bearish sentiment on bond and stock markets. \par \par \uc2  Treasury bill rates are up four to five points, with a compounded maximum annual rate at 130%. \par \par \uc2  At about 1151 GMT, the Istanbul Stock Exchange main IMKB-100 index was at 2584 points, down 412.36 points or 13.8% on the day. \par \par \uc2  Traders said lira positions are being cashed and switched into dollars. \par \par \uc2  An Istanbul-based trader with a foreign bank said there was also some dollar demand from retail investors, a development that hasn't seen before. \par \par \uc2  -By Selim Atalay +90 312 512 5017; satalay@ap.org. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 27-08-98 \par \par \uc2  1215GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8r07am1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\