\par\par \b \uc2  Turk cenbank funds cash via money mkt, \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1998\par 16:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  1400 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lent 98.8 trillion lira at 76 percent on overnight and 1.5 trillion tomorrow/next money market deals due to severe cash squeeze stemmed from corporate tax payments. \par \par \uc2  * The bank also injected cash via two rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell lira for dollars during the day. \par \par \uc2  * Average overnight money rate rose to 75.94 percent from 75.37 percent. \par \par \uc2  * Bond yields also rose by around 2.5-3 percentage points to a range of 88-91 percent in line with cash squeeze and firmer cost of funding indicated by overnight repo rates. \par \par \uc2  * Average overnight repo rates also jumped to 80.23 percent from Monday's 66.08 on tax-free deals and to 85.98 percent from 73.80 on taxed deals. \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL, Aug 4 (Reuters) - Corporate tax payments caused a cash squeeze in Turkey's money market on Tuesday, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fund banks in the money and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The bank injected 98.8 trillion lira on overnight deals and 1.5 trillion lira in tomorrow/next money interbank deals at 76 percent after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling lira for dollars. \par \par \uc2  The market was 104 trillion lira short on Tuesday, causing the average overnight money rate to rise to 75.94 percent from 75.37 percent previously. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tomorrow/next funding followed heavy treasury borrowing in an auction of 359.1 trillion lira of six-month bills compared with repayment of around 266.8 trillion lira, bankers said. \par \par \uc2  The cash squeeze and the firmer cost of funding pushed bond yields up by around 2.5-3 percentage points. \par \par \uc2  The yields on the most active papers rose to 91.8 and 88.28 percent from 89.15 and 85.17 percent respectively. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the last auctioned February 3, 1999 paper rose to 88.19 from 86.90 percent on tomorrow value deals. \par \par \uc2  The average repo rates, indicating the cost of bond funding, jumped to 80.23 percent from 66.08 on tax-free deals and to 85.98 percent from 73.80 percent on taxed deals. \par \par \uc2  ((Istanbul newsroom, +90-212-275 0875, fax +90-212-267 2137, istanbul.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8400xwe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
