\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey Bank Dlr Selling -3: Closing of Dlr Positions Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 1997\par 14:52\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The dollar at the interbank market closed Monday at 135,100 Turkish lira after a series of dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said major Turkish banks are buying dollars, which - given the lira shortage - might indicate that political instability is prompting banks to cover some short dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Excluding major state banks, private banks' short dollar positions are seen about $2.5 billion, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves $15 billion to $16 billion. \par \par \uc2  A trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Monday sold lira at the repo market, possibly with the aim of drawing off this liquidity through dollar sales, which also subdue the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, another Istanbul-based trader said the lira selling might not be a smart move, as banks tend to buy dollars with it. \par \par \uc2  'As the political instability continues, buying pressure on the dollar will continue,' he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $300 million to $400 million Monday on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The lira's depreciation rate in April against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket of 1 dollar and 1.5 Deutsche marks is seen about 4.6%, traders said. They estimated the monthly inflation rate at 5% to 6%. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expect the lira demand to continue until May 7 and the Turkish Treasury to hold a bill auction on its repayment of 129.9 trillion lira, due that day. \par \par \uc2  Participants are unsure whether the treasury will attempt an inflation-indexed bill auction or a zero-coupon auction. \par \par \uc2  Given Monday's activity in the market, dollar buying demand is expected to continue Tuesday, inviting new \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 28-04-97 \par \par \uc2  1252GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt4s02yer\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
