\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's Erbakan/Markets -2: Markets Seen To Recover \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1997\par 09:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The political crisis that started last Friday have hit the markets hard as the t-bill rates jumped more than 10 percentage points -- the Istanbul Stock Exchange shed 15 points and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was the target of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With a calmer mood, participants expect some recovery for Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Markets were annoyed that the Islamist prime minister Erbakan was on a collision course with the generals who believe Erbakan and his party are eroding the fundamentals of the secular pro-western republic. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's newspapers report that Erbakan will sign the statement of the National Security Council where generals expressed their concerns about anti-secularism and asked the government to act against it. The measures recommended by the generals are designed to upset Erbakan's constituents. \par \par \uc2  'The crisis is gone with the wind,' said Ertan Mertoglu of a manager at Yasarbank's treasury unit. 'I'm now wondering whether there was really such big crisis, or we the market participants were exaggerating it.' \par \par \uc2  Overall, participants said major players' and especially commercial banks' vested interests are for calmer and less volatile markets. Since Friday. markets were concerned that Erbakan's defiance of the army could lead to the collapse of the government and lead to a period of political uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  With Erbakan's signature, crisis isn't completely over as the Council's recommendations will be debated in Thursday's cabinet meeting. Analysts said PM Erbakan might find it hard to explain to his party why he signed the statement which calls him to contradict with his earlier policies. \par \par \uc2  'Power is always sweeter,' said Yasarbank's Mertoglu who said the government will try to stay in power as long \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 06-03-97 \par \par \uc2  0806GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt3601ksy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
