\par\par \b \uc2  Turkey's Lira Down Vs Dlr In A Lira Demanding Market \b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 1997\par 12:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  ISTANBUL (AP-Dow Jones)--The Turkish lira is lower against the dollar Tuesday in a calmer and lira demanding market, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They added that the market is still uneasy about the dollar's cross gains, but dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Turkey\b0 \highlight\uc2  have subsided the market for now. The market also anticipates a correction at the dollar/Deutsche mark crosses. \par \par \uc2  'The bank sold around 400 to 500 million dollars,' an Istanbul bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager said. 'The amount is good enough to calm the market. Of course we don't know what will happen in case of a constant demand by 5 or 6 banks. But we're not at that point.' \par \par \uc2  Around 1000 GMT, the dollar bids at the dollar/lira interbank market were at 121,040 lira, up from Monday's close at 120,750 lira and Monday's two \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar buying rate for commercial banks' minimum reserve requirements were at 120,800 lira, up from Monday's close at 120,550 lira. The lira interbank market opened with a reserve of minus 26.4 trillion lira. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that banks' demand of the lira and the lira interbank market lending rates of over 71% are curbing any dollar buying demand. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold dollars since last Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  'Banks have no interest to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve at this point,' said a trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's international gains continue to tempt investors who aren't happy with the short term interest rates. They added that banks are not keen in extending their short dollar positions and they are quick to replace any dollars they sell to their customers. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 18-02-97 \par \par \uc2  1116GMT \par \par \uc2  1169 1862 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt2i00yxm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
